  <p>A stalled squat is frustrating. Strength coach Jordan Hoppel starts with recovery and the nervous system before anything else.</p>

  <h2>The main reasons</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Overtrained.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Under-recovered.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>An underlying injury</strong> they don't realize is holding them back.</li>
    <li><strong>Genetic limit.</strong> Possible, but rare outside professional lifters or bodybuilders.</li>
  </ul>

  <JordanQuote>Generally, their nervous system is fatigued.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>If you're a beginner</h2>
  <p>Beginner stalls are usually form-related. Common flaws are not positioning the body correctly, not using the correct muscles in the right order or context, movements that aren't coordinated or smooth, or being in an open-loop movement pattern when a closed-loop one is needed.</p>

  <Media type="video" youtubeId="" alt="Jordan coaching squat depth and positioning" brief="Short video: Jordan walks through squat positioning faults that stall beginners." />

  <h2>What to do about it</h2>
  <p>If you're recovered and it's a pure training plateau, move to training that challenges the nervous system. Jordan's squat plan from 225 toward 315 shows how: <a href="/how-to-squat-315-from-225/">read the full progression</a>. If you suspect fatigue, review <a href="/what-is-a-deload-week/">how to run a deload</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
